/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Filters Filters
 * @ingroup DspCore
 * @brief Single-rate FIR/IIR filters, coefficient designers, and future filter families.
 *
 * FIR<SampleType, CoefficientType, StateType> and
 * IIR<SampleType, CoefficientType, StateType> own their coefficient and delay
 * storage. SampleType is the external stream type; CoefficientType defaults to
 * float and StateType defaults to CoefficientType. Configure may resize storage
 * outside the processing path; Process never allocates. Both accept direct,
 * caller-owned `Processing::SampleBlock<const SampleType>` input and
 * `Processing::SampleBlock<SampleType>` output views with the same fixed
 * extent. Biquad functions are
 * stateless coefficient designers: each returns a coefficient value accepted by
 * IIR::Configure. Use Biquad::Design<CoefficientType> to choose coefficient
 * precision explicitly; it defaults to float. Biquad::ParameterCount(Type),
 * Biquad::ParameterAt(Type, cpstd::size_t, ParameterInfo&) and
 * Biquad::HasParameter(Type, Parameter) expose allocation-free metadata for
 * controls required by each design. The metadata remains in program memory on
 * AVR and is copied into a caller-owned value on access.
 *
 * The implemented processors are under @ref DspCore_Filters_SingleRate.
 * `SOS.h`, `Multirate.h`, and `MIMO.h` establish future module boundaries;
 * they deliberately declare no incomplete runtime API.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Filters_Equations Difference equations and coefficients
 * Both processors apply a discrete-time difference equation one sample at a
 * time. In this documentation @f$x[n]@f$ is the input sample at index @f$n@f$
 * and @f$y[n]@f$ is the corresponding output sample. `Process` visits a block
 * in increasing @f$n@f$ order; it is not a vectorized evaluation of a
 * memoryless function @f$Y(x)@f$.
 *
 * FIR accepts taps @f$b_k@f$ and performs convolution:
 * @f[
 * y[n] = \sum_{k=0}^{N-1}b_kx[n-k], \qquad
 * H(z) = \sum_{k=0}^{N-1}b_kz^{-k}.
 * @f]
 * The class stores only earlier input samples. IIR accepts numerator @f$b_k@f$
 * and denominator @f$a_k@f$ coefficients:
 * @f[
 * \sum_{k=0}^{N}a_ky[n-k] = \sum_{k=0}^{M}b_kx[n-k], \qquad
 * H(z) = \frac{\sum b_kz^{-k}}{\sum a_kz^{-k}}.
 * @f]
 * It stores earlier inputs and outputs. IIR::Configure divides all
 * coefficients by @f$a_0@f$, after which the code computes the feed-forward
 * sum and subtracts the feedback sum. See FIR and IIR for the exact mapping
 * from these equations to their delay lines and arithmetic types.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Filters_Biquad RBJ biquad coefficient design
 *
 * Biquad's stateless designers return the unnormalized second-order form
 * @f[
 * H(z)=\frac{b_0+b_1z^{-1}+b_2z^{-2}}
 *            {a_0+a_1z^{-1}+a_2z^{-2}}.
 * @f]
 * IIR::Configure subsequently normalizes every coefficient by @f$a_0@f$.
 * For sample rate @f$f_s@f$, cutoff or center frequency @f$f_0@f$, and
 * quality @f$Q@f$, the shared RBJ terms are
 * @f[
 * \omega_0=2\pi\frac{f_0}{f_s}, \qquad
 * c=\cos\omega_0, \qquad
 * \alpha=\frac{\sin\omega_0}{2Q}.
 * @f]
 * Low-pass and high-pass use, respectively,
 * @f[
 * (b_0,b_1,b_2)=\left(\frac{1-c}{2},1-c,\frac{1-c}{2}\right),
 * \qquad (a_0,a_1,a_2)=(1+\alpha,-2c,1-\alpha),
 * @f]
 * @f[
 * (b_0,b_1,b_2)=\left(\frac{1+c}{2},-(1+c),\frac{1+c}{2}\right),
 * \qquad (a_0,a_1,a_2)=(1+\alpha,-2c,1-\alpha).
 * @f]
 * Band-pass, notch, and all-pass use
 * @f[
 * \begin{array}{c|c}
 * \mathrm{BandPass}&(\alpha,0,-\alpha)\\
 * \mathrm{Notch}&(1,-2c,1)\\
 * \mathrm{AllPass}&(1-\alpha,-2c,1+\alpha)
 * \end{array}
 * \qquad
 * (a_0,a_1,a_2)=(1+\alpha,-2c,1-\alpha).
 * @f]
 * Peak EQ additionally uses @f$A=10^{G/40}@f$ for gain @f$G@f$ in dB:
 * @f[
 * (b_0,b_1,b_2)=(1+\alpha A,-2c,1-\alpha A), \qquad
 * (a_0,a_1,a_2)=\left(1+\frac{\alpha}{A},-2c,1-\frac{\alpha}{A}\right).
 * @f]
 * Shelf designs use @f$A=10^{G/40}@f$, slope @f$S@f$, and
 * @f[
 * \alpha=\frac{\sin\omega_0}{2}
 * \sqrt{\left(A+\frac1A\right)\left(\frac1S-1\right)+2},
 * \qquad \beta=2\sqrt A\,\alpha.
 * @f]
 * Their low/high shelf coefficient forms are the RBJ equations implemented by
 * Biquad::LowShelf and Biquad::HighShelf; the returned six values remain the
 * exact source of truth for a configured IIR section.
 */

/**
 * @namespace DspCore::Filters
 * @ingroup DspCore_Filters
 * @brief Block processors and coefficient-design namespaces.
 */
